=========================================================
BUILDER DESIGN PATTERN (C++ INTERVIEW NOTES)
=========================================================

Pattern Type:
--------------
Creational Design Pattern

Purpose:
--------
Construct a complex object step-by-step while separating
the construction process from its representation.

Main Principle:
---------------
Separate object construction from object representation.

Priority:
---------
⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐ MUST KNOW FOR C++ LLD INTERVIEWS


=========================================================
1. WHAT IS BUILDER PATTERN?
=========================================================

Builder is a creational design pattern used when an object
requires multiple construction steps.

Instead of creating the object directly using a large
constructor, the object is built one step at a time.

CORE IDEA:

Client should NOT know how a complex object is built.

Builder is responsible for constructing the object.

FLOW:

Client
   |
Director
   |
Builder Interface
   |
Concrete Builder
   |
Product


=========================================================
2. WHY DO WE NEED BUILDER?
=========================================================

Suppose we need to create a Car.

A Car has:

- Engine
- Body
- Seats
- Airbags
- GPS
- Sunroof
- Music System

Without Builder:

Car car("V8",
        "Coupe",
        "Leather",
        true,
        true,
        false,
        ...);

Problems:

• Constructor becomes very large.
• Difficult to remember parameter order.
• Many optional parameters.
• Hard to read.
• Hard to maintain.
• Difficult to create different configurations.

Builder solves this by constructing the object
step-by-step.


=========================================================
3. WITHOUT BUILDER (PROBLEM)
=========================================================

#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Car
{
public:

    string engine;
    string body;
    string seats;

    Car(string e,
        string b,
        string s)
    {
        engine = e;
        body = b;
        seats = s;
    }

    void show()
    {
        cout << engine << " "
             << body << " "
             << seats << endl;
    }
};

int main()
{
    Car sports("V8",
               "Coupe",
               "2 Sports Seats");

    sports.show();

    Car luxury("V6 Turbo",
               "Sedan",
               "5 Leather Seats");

    luxury.show();
}

=========================================================
PROBLEMS
=========================================================

1. Large constructors.

2. Difficult to remember parameter order.

3. Optional parameters make constructors huge.

4. Construction logic is duplicated.

5. Hard to create multiple configurations.

6. Difficult to maintain.


=========================================================
4. HOW BUILDER SOLVES THE PROBLEM
=========================================================

Instead of:

Client -> new Car(...)

We do:

Client
   |
Director
   |
Builder
   |
Car

The Builder constructs each part separately.

Example:

builder->setEngine();

builder->setBody();

builder->setSeats();

Director controls the sequence.


=========================================================
5. STRUCTURE
=========================================================

Builder Pattern has 5 participants.


1. Product
----------

Object being created.

Example:

Car


2. Builder Interface
--------------------

Defines construction steps.

Example:

setEngine()

setBody()

setSeats()


3. Concrete Builder
-------------------

Implements construction logic.

Example:

SportsCarBuilder

LuxuryCarBuilder


4. Director
-----------

Controls construction order.

Example:

buildCar()


5. Client
---------

Chooses the builder and starts construction.


=========================================================
6. C++ IMPLEMENTATION
=========================================================

#include <iostream>
#include <string>
using namespace std;

=========================================================
PRODUCT
=========================================================

class Car
{
public:

    string engine;
    string body;
    string seats;

    void show()
    {
        cout << "Engine : " << engine << endl;
        cout << "Body   : " << body << endl;
        cout << "Seats  : " << seats << endl;
    }
};

=========================================================
BUILDER INTERFACE
=========================================================

class CarBuilder
{
public:

    virtual void setEngine() = 0;

    virtual void setBody() = 0;

    virtual void setSeats() = 0;

    virtual Car* getCar() = 0; ***************

    virtual ~CarBuilder() {}
};

=========================================================
CONCRETE BUILDER
=========================================================

class SportsCarBuilder : public CarBuilder
{
private:

    Car* car; *********

public:

    SportsCarBuilder()
    {
        car = new Car();
    }

    void setEngine() override
    {
        car->engine = "V8";
    }

    void setBody() override
    {
        car->body = "Coupe";
    }

    void setSeats() override
    {
        car->seats = "2 Sports Seats";
    }

    Car* getCar() override
    {
        return car;
    }
};

class LuxuryCarBuilder : public CarBuilder
{
private:

    Car* car;

public:

    LuxuryCarBuilder()
    {
        car = new Car();
    }

    void setEngine() override
    {
        car->engine = "V6 Turbo";
    }

    void setBody() override
    {
        car->body = "Sedan";
    }

    void setSeats() override
    {
        car->seats = "5 Leather Seats";
    }

    Car* getCar() override
    {
        return car;
    }
};

=========================================================
DIRECTOR
=========================================================

class CarDirector
{
private:

    CarBuilder* builder;

public:

    CarDirector(CarBuilder* b)
    {
        builder = b;
    }

    Car* buildCar()
    {
        builder->setEngine();
        builder->setBody();
        builder->setSeats();

        return builder->getCar();
    }
};

=========================================================
CLIENT
=========================================================

int main()
{
    SportsCarBuilder sportsBuilder;

    CarDirector director(&sportsBuilder);

    Car* sports = director.buildCar();

    sports->show();

    cout << endl;

    LuxuryCarBuilder luxuryBuilder;

    CarDirector director2(&luxuryBuilder);

    Car* luxury = director2.buildCar();

    luxury->show();

    delete sports;
    delete luxury;
}

